{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n    if is_palindrome(string):\n        return string\n\n    # As per the design document's \"System Implementation\" and \"Sequence Diagram\",\n    # we iteratively append characters from the reverse of the input string to form a suffix.\n    reversed_input = string[::-1]\n    suffix_parts = []\n\n    for i in range(len(reversed_input)):\n        suffix_parts.append(reversed_input[i])\n        current_suffix = \"\".join(suffix_parts)\n        candidate_string = string + current_suffix\n        if is_palindrome(candidate_string):\n            return candidate_string\n\n    # This line should logically only be reached if the input string is empty (handled above),\n    # or if for some reason string + reversed_input itself wasn't a palindrome.\n    # For any non-empty string, string + string[::-1] is always a palindrome,\n    # and the loop would have found it (or an earlier, shorter one).\n    return string + reversed_input\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n    >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result = []\n    current_max = float('-inf')\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_max = max(current_max, number)\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result"}
