{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "```python\ndef is_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"Return true if a given number is prime, and false otherwise.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of n.\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "```python\ndef is_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"Return true if a given number is prime, and false otherwise.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        # Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not prime.\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:\n        # 2 and 3 are prime numbers.\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        # Exclude multiples of 2 or 3.\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    Returns xs[0] + xs[1]*x + xs[2]*x^2 + ... + xs[n]*x^n.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a single zero point for a polynomial given its coefficients xs.\n    It assumes input validity (even number of coefficients, largest non-zero) and uses a simple search.\n    \"\"\"\n    epsilon = 1e-6\n\n    # Handle linear polynomials directly: ax + b = 0 => x = -b/a\n    if len(xs) == 2:\n        return -xs[0] / xs[1]\n\n    # For higher degree polynomials, perform a simple iterative search around zero.\n    step_size = 0.001\n    search_limit = 50.0\n\n    x_current = 0.0\n    # Search positive direction\n    while x_current <= search_limit:\n        if abs(poly(xs, x_current)) < epsilon:\n            return x_current\n        x_current += step_size\n\n    x_current = -step_size\n    # Search negative direction\n    while x_current >= -search_limit:\n        if abs(poly(xs, x_current)) < epsilon:\n            return x_current\n        x_current -= step_size\n\n    # The problem guarantees a solution; this return is a fallback and implies\n    # the root exists outside the search_limit or between step_size increments.\n    return 0.0\n```"}
