{"task_id": "HumanEval/58", "solution": "```python\ndef common(l1: list, l2: list):\n    \"\"\"Return sorted unique common elements for two lists using set intersection.\n    This method efficiently finds unique commonalities and then sorts them.\n    \"\"\"\n    set1 = set(l1)\n    set2 = set(l2)\n    common_set = set1.intersection(set2)\n    result_list = list(common_set)\n    result_list.sort()\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "def largest_prime_factor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n.\n    Assumes n > 1 and is not a prime number.\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(13195)\n    29\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(2048)\n    2\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_prime = 1\n\n    # Divide by 2 until n is odd\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest_prime = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Check for odd factors from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n    factor = 3\n    while factor * factor <= n:\n        while n % factor == 0:\n            largest_prime = factor\n            n //= factor\n        factor += 2 # Move to the next odd number\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        largest_prime = n\n\n    return largest_prime"}
