{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\ndef _tokenize_string(s):\n    \"\"\"Tokenizes the input string into a list of words and contiguous space sequences.\n    This helper function ensures spaces are preserved as separate tokens.\"\"\"\n    tokens = []\n    if not s:\n        return tokens\n\n    current_token_chars = []\n    # Determine if the first character is a space to set initial state\n    is_current_token_space = s[0].isspace()\n\n    for char in s:\n        # Check if the character type (space/non-space) matches the current token's type\n        if char.isspace() == is_current_token_space:\n            current_token_chars.append(char)\n        else:\n            # Type changed: finish the current token, add it to tokens, and start a new one\n            tokens.append(\"\".join(current_token_chars))\n            current_token_chars = [char]\n            is_current_token_space = char.isspace()\n\n    # Add the last accumulated token\n    tokens.append(\"\".join(current_token_chars))\n    return tokens\n\ndef _sort_word_characters(word):\n    \"\"\"Sorts the characters of a given word in ascending ASCII order.\n    It returns the new string with sorted characters.\"\"\"\n    return \"\".join(sorted(word))\n\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function that takes a string and returns an ordered version of it.\n    Ordered version of string, is a string where all words (separated by space)\n    are replaced by a new word where all the characters arranged in\n    ascending order based on ascii value.\n    Note: You should keep the order of words and blank spaces in the sentence.\n\n    For example:\n    anti_shuffle('Hi') returns 'Hi'\n    anti_shuffle('hello') returns 'ehllo'\n    anti_shuffle('Hello World!!!') returns 'Hello !!!Wdlor'\n    \"\"\"\n    processed_tokens = []\n    tokens = _tokenize_string(s)\n\n    for token in tokens:\n        # If a token consists only of spaces, preserve it as is.\n        if token.isspace():\n            processed_tokens.append(token)\n        # Otherwise, the token is a word, so sort its characters.\n        else:\n            processed_tokens.append(_sort_word_characters(token))\n\n    # Reconstruct the final string by joining all processed tokens.\n    return \"\".join(processed_tokens)\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/91", "solution": "class _Sentence:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates a sentence's content and logic to check if it starts with 'I'.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, content: str):\n        self._content = content\n\n    def starts_with_i(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Returns True if the sentence starts with the standalone word \"I\", False otherwise.\"\"\"\n        cleaned_content = self._content.strip()\n        return cleaned_content.startswith(\"I \") or cleaned_content == \"I\"\n\ndef _split_into_sentences(text: str, delimiters: str = \".?!\") -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Splits a given text into a list of raw sentence strings based on delimiters.\"\"\"\n    sentences = []\n    current_sentence_start = 0\n    for i, char in enumerate(text):\n        if char in delimiters:\n            sentence_content = text[current_sentence_start:i]\n            sentences.append(sentence_content)\n            current_sentence_start = i + 1\n\n    if current_sentence_start < len(text):\n        sentences.append(text[current_sentence_start:])\n\n    return [s for s in sentences if s.strip()]\n\ndef _count_boredoms(sentences: list[_Sentence]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Iterates through a list of _Sentence objects and counts those that start with 'I'.\"\"\"\n    boredom_count = 0\n    for sentence_obj in sentences:\n        if sentence_obj.starts_with_i():\n            boredom_count += 1\n    return boredom_count\n\ndef is_bored(S: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts sentences starting with the standalone word \"I\".\n    Sentences are delimited by '.', '?' or '!'.\n    \"\"\"\n    raw_sentences = _split_into_sentences(S, \".?!\")\n    sentence_objects = [_Sentence(raw_s) for raw_s in raw_sentences]\n    return _count_boredoms(sentence_objects)"}
